1. Executive Summary

This project focuses on predicting customer churn for a retail loyalty program using customer profile, purchasing, engagement, and support data. The dataset contains approximately 2,600 customers covering the period from January 2023 to June 2024. A leakage-safe modelling approach was used, with features generated up to March 2024 and churn defined from customer activity during April–June 2024. Multiple machine-learning models were evaluated, with HistGradientBoosting achieving the strongest validation performance.

2. Business Problem and Objective

Customer churn can reduce the long-term value of a retail loyalty program. The objective of this project is to identify customers at higher risk of churn and understand the behavioural factors associated with that risk.

The analysis focuses on:

Identifying behavioural and engagement patterns related to churn.
Comparing churn risk across membership tiers, cities, and signup cohorts.
Building a predictive model that produces customer-level churn probabilities.
Explaining model predictions using SHAP.
Simulating a potential retention intervention for high-risk customers.3. Exploratory Data Analysis
3.1 Churn Distribution

The cleaned modelling dataset contains 2,600 customers:

Churn Status	Customers	Percentage
Retained	1,782	68.54%
Churned	818	31.46%
Total	2,600	100%

The target variable shows moderate class imbalance, with churned customers representing approximately 31.5% of the modelling population. This imbalance was considered during model evaluation, with emphasis placed on precision, recall, F1-score and PR-AUC rather than accuracy alone.

3.2 Churn by Membership Tier
Membership Tier	Customers	Observed Churn Rate
Gold	824	30.10%
Platinum	302	31.13%
Silver	1,474	32.29%

Churn rates across membership tiers are relatively close. Silver customers have the highest observed churn rate at 32.29%, while Gold has the lowest at 30.10%. The relatively small differences indicate that membership tier alone does not provide sufficient information for identifying churn risk.

3.3 Churn by City

The observed churn rate varies across the ten cities in the dataset.

Austin and Columbus exhibit comparatively high observed churn rates of 37.59% and 36.19%, respectively. Portland and Spokane have lower observed churn rates of 28.33% and 28.97%. These differences are descriptive segment-level patterns and should not be interpreted as evidence that location causes churn.

