3.7 EDA Summary

Add this short paragraph at the end:

The EDA indicates that behavioural engagement is more strongly differentiated between retained and churned customers than basic demographic or membership attributes. Customers who churned generally showed lower purchasing frequency, spending and digital engagement, together with somewhat higher support and complaint activity. These observations motivated the inclusion of RFM, engagement and support features in the predictive modelling stage.

4. Feature Engineering
4.1 Prediction and Label Windows

To prevent target leakage, the modelling features were constructed only from information available before the churn-label period.

Period	Dates	Purpose
Feature window	Jan 2023 – Mar 2024	Used to construct predictors
Label window	Apr 2024 – Jun 2024	Used to define the supplied churn outcome

The feature cutoff was:

FEATURE_END = 2024-03-31

and the label window was:

LABEL_START = 2024-04-01
LABEL_END   = 2024-06-30

Activity records from April–June 2024 were excluded from feature construction because they belong to the outcome period. This prevents information about the customer's future churn behaviour from being directly incorporated into the predictors.

This directly addresses the assessment requirement that features use information available before the label window.

4.2 Feature Groups

The final customer-level modelling dataset contained 31 predictive features before preprocessing.

A. Customer Profile Features

These included:

Age
Gender
City
Membership tier
Marketing opt-in
Preferred store
Preferred category
Home-store price tier
Signup year
Signup month
Customer tenure
B. RFM / Purchasing Features

The following purchasing features were derived from the pre-label activity window:

total_transactions
total_spend
avg_basket_value
active_months
observed_months
RECENCY_MONTHS
NO_HISTORICAL_PURCHASE
NO_PRELABEL_ACTIVITY

Recency was calculated as the number of months between the feature cutoff and the customer's most recent positive-transaction month.

For customers with no historical purchase, a sentinel value was used together with a separate indicator variable.

C. Engagement Features

The model included:

Average app sessions
Total app sessions
Average emails opened
Total emails opened
Total coupons redeemed
Average distinct categories
Average promotional transaction percentage
D. Support Features

Support-related predictors included:

Total support tickets
Average support tickets
Complaint count
Complaint rate
Whether the customer had any complaint
4.3 Handling Customers Without Pre-Label Activity

Some customers had no activity records in the pre-label feature window.

Instead of removing these customers, an explicit:

NO_PRELABEL_ACTIVITY

indicator was created.

Their activity-based numerical features were represented as zero where appropriate.

This approach preserves the full customer population while allowing the model to distinguish between customers with genuinely low activity and customers for whom no pre-label activity record was available.

4.4 Missing-Value Handling

Missing values were handled within the modelling preprocessing pipeline.

Numerical features:

Median imputation was applied using the training data.

Categorical features:

Missing categorical values were imputed using the most frequent training-category value, followed by one-hot encoding with unknown-category handling.

The original missingness in LAST_PURCHASE_DATE was preserved during data preparation and was not used as a predictive feature, because it is part of the supplied label/history information.

4.5 Final Modelling Dataset

After joining the cleaned customer profile, activity-derived features and supplied churn labels:

Customers: 2,600
Predictive features: 31
Final dataset columns: 35

The target distribution was:

Target	Customers	Percentage
Retained (0)	1,782	68.54%
Churned (1)	818	31.46%

5. Validation Strategy
5.1 Chronological Cohort Split

A chronological validation strategy was used instead of a random train/test split.

Dataset	Signup Cohort	Customers
Training	2021–2022	1,537
Validation	2023	816
Final Test	2024	247

The 2021–2022 customers were used for model training, 2023 customers were used for validation and model/threshold selection, and the 2024 cohort was retained as the final temporal holdout.

This approach better reflects a prospective prediction setting because the model is evaluated on a later customer cohort rather than randomly mixing customers across time. The assessment specifically calls for time/cohort-aware validation.

5.2 Why the 2024 Cohort Requires Caution

The 2024 cohort contains only 4 observed churners out of 247 customers.

Additionally, the 2024 cohort had substantially shorter observed histories than earlier cohorts.

Therefore:

Performance metrics on the 2024 holdout are useful as a temporal generalization check but should be interpreted cautiously because the number of positive churn cases is very small.

Section 6 — Model Development & Results
6.1 Models Evaluated

Three classification models were evaluated:

Logistic Regression — baseline model
Random Forest — tree-based ensemble
HistGradientBoosting — gradient-boosting model

Class weighting was used for Logistic Regression and Random Forest to account for the churn-class imbalance.

6.2 Validation Results
Model	Accuracy	Precision	Recall	F1	PR-AUC
Logistic Regression	40.69%	32.07%	99.56%	48.51%	41.88%
Random Forest	54.90%	38.28%	99.13%	55.23%	93.48%
HistGradientBoosting	88.97%	75.09%	90.83%	82.21%	93.82%

HistGradientBoosting provided the strongest validation performance, achieving an F1 score of 82.21% and PR-AUC of 93.82%.

Insert: outputs/figures/model_comparison.png

6.3 Threshold Analysis

The default threshold of 0.50 was evaluated along with alternative thresholds using the 2023 validation cohort. The threshold of 0.91 produced the highest validation F1 score of approximately 87.56%.

However, threshold selection was not based only on F1 because the final 2024 test cohort contained only four observed churners.

6.4 Final 2024 Holdout

At threshold 0.50:

Metric	Result
Accuracy	98.38%
Precision	50.00%
Recall	100.00%
F1	66.67%
PR-AUC	76.79%

7. Explainability — SHAP

SHAP was used to identify which features contributed most strongly to the HistGradientBoosting predictions.

Rank	Feature	Mean |SHAP|
1	RECENCY_MONTHS	4.105
2	total_transactions	0.846
3	observed_months	0.600
4	total_app_sessions	0.438
5	avg_emails_opened	0.391

Purchase recency was the strongest model driver, followed by purchase frequency and historical engagement. App and email engagement were also important predictive signals. These relationships represent model associations and should not be interpreted as causal effects.

Insert: outputs/figures/shap_global_importance.png

8. Ablation Study

The contribution of different feature groups was evaluated:

Feature Set	F1	PR-AUC
Profile Only	28.21%	27.88%
Profile + RFM	69.90%	91.91%
Full Behavioural	82.21%	93.82%

The results show that behavioural information provides substantially more predictive signal than profile variables alone. RFM features produced a large improvement, while additional engagement and support features further improved performance.

9. Segment Risk Analysis
Membership Tier

Silver customers had the highest average predicted risk (37.57%) and observed churn rate (32.29%).

City

Columbus had the highest average predicted risk (41.41%), followed by Austin (40.90%). Austin had the highest observed churn rate (37.59%).

Signup Cohort

The 2021 and 2022 cohorts had observed churn rates of 37.45% and 38.64%, respectively. The 2024 cohort had much lower observed churn, but its shorter history and high insufficient-history proportion make direct comparison unreliable.

10. Retention Scenario

An illustrative retention scenario was created using:

Intervention cost: 10 units/customer
Assumed success rate: 20%
Assumed customer value: 100 units
Strategy	Customers Targeted	Expected Retained	Estimated Net Impact
Probability ≥ 0.50	8	1.6	80
Top 10% Risk	25	5.0	250
Top 20% Risk	50	10.0	500

These are illustrative scenario estimates based on assumed parameters and should not be interpreted as observed financial returns.

11. Recommendations
Prioritize purchase recency and frequency when identifying customers whose engagement is declining.
Use behavioural risk scores to support targeted retention rather than applying the same intervention to every customer.
Monitor digital engagement, particularly app sessions and email engagement.
Review customers with repeated support or complaint activity as part of retention monitoring.
Separate insufficient-history customers from established customers because limited observation can make their risk estimates less reliable.
12. Limitations
The 2024 test cohort contains only 4 observed churners, making test metrics sensitive to individual cases.
The 2024 signup cohort has substantially shorter customer histories.
The retention scenario uses assumed intervention costs, success rates and customer value.
Observed relationships and SHAP associations indicate predictive patterns, not causation.
The model should be re-evaluated on future cohorts before being treated as a production system.
13. Conclusion

This project developed a leakage-safe customer churn prediction framework using customer profile, purchasing, engagement and support information. HistGradientBoosting achieved an F1 score of 82.21% and PR-AUC of 93.82% on the validation cohort. SHAP analysis identified purchase recency, transaction frequency and historical engagement as the strongest predictive factors, while the ablation study demonstrated the substantial value of behavioural features. The resulting customer-level risk scores and retention scenario provide a foundation for targeted customer retention analysis.